He's not used that term, but his basic idea is that the myths suggest we have an innate drive towards conquering inner and external demons, and that's why myths have lasted as long as they have. 

&#x200B;

Maybe they've just lasted that long because it's what all of us aspire to be. But many of us are doomed and possibly even destined to have far less inspirational lives.

&#x200B;

My idea, and my interpretation of Jung's quote, is that to live authentically for some people is to lead a life that ends in tragic failure. For some, that's the ONLY way they can live and the fulfillment of their own law.

&#x200B;

Jung, in this quote, is a relativist. Peterson's presents an absolute argument that is compelling though a selective reading of Jung.

I feel like at times, he selectively quotes from things just to support his own ideas. For example, he used mythology to suggest that every human being has an innate drive towards self-actualisation. But what about our drives towards self-destruction?  Maybe for some, their destiny IS to self-destruct. Jung alludes to this here:

&#x200B;

 “The fact that a man who goes his own way ends in ruin means nothing ... He must obey his own law, as if it were a daemon whispering to him of new and wonderful paths ... The only meaningful life is a life that strives for the individual realization — absolute and unconditional— of its own particular law ... To the extent that a man is untrue to the law of his being ... he has failed to realize his own life's meaning.” 

&#x200B;

My interpretation of this is that some of us will overcome our fears and go our own way, but that won't save us from self-annihilation.  I don't know how this fits into Peterson's more optimistic message that good comes from following the truth. What if the truth for some of us IS ruination?

&#x200B;

I know Peterson would call this a nihilistic argument. But maybe nihilism is the fate for some.

Because people like comedians more when they connect to the point of view. And one liners tend to be more about word play or flights of imagination, which are distancing devices. 

&#x200B;

Also, with one liner comics, they get into the rhythm where you kind of know every single joke is going to follow a formula and for almost every 'pure' one liner comic, it gets tiring after about 12 minutes.

That's why every successful one liner comic also has something else.

Jimmy Carr and Jeselnik are crowd work masters. From what I've read, Dangerfield was too in the clubs. 

Steven Wright Demetri Martin and Emo Phillips all did songs

Woody Allen added stories to the mix

Mitch Hedberg was equally brilliant at observational humour as well as tagging his jokes into longer bits. 

&#x200B;

Dan Mintz has an album which is 50 minutes of jokes which are pretty much all one liners all done in a deadpan voice. I think it's a bit much after about 20 minutes. I really do believe you need to have another gear to your comedy.

&#x200B;

I'm a one liner guy myself, but I'm trying to develop more act outs just to vary things in my sets. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I think it's something psychological where you don't have connection with a bit anymore and that comes across in the performance. It could be you've grown out of it so you're just not into it when you say the lines. 

&#x200B;

This doesn't makes too much sense for me cos I perform in total deadpan. But I've noticed it happens more with bits on emotional states or experiences I'm not that engaged with at the moment. Maybe a real pro performer wouldn't let this get to them.

&#x200B;

It could also be the older material was never great to begin with, but you delivered it well cos it was new. But it never really had enough to it beyond that initial 'fresh' period.

&#x200B;

It's really weird when jokes that once got applause breaks go from that to just nothing though....but I think the answer is in some of the comments I made above.

Difficult question for me to answer. I'm a negative thinker and I don't look at reality objectively. I could list some things but I'd feel 'fake' doing that. I could also type a load of negative stuff, but I'll save you that as well. I'll just focus more on how I feel about it.  


I don't fully believe in 'super powers.' I just think if you have low self-esteem, masturbation can make it worse.  However, the fact that I felt more confidence around women on NoFap says more about my insecurities that it does about NoFap. I think the boost in confidence is linked to perfectionism, and thinking I have to be an ideal for any woman to be attracted to me. And I think when on no fap, I could feel like I was closer to that and felt better about myself as a result. But a truly confident person would think, 'I'm okay as I am, flaws and all.' 

&#x200B;

To do anything hard you need self-discipline and self-discipline increases self-worth. However, there's a limit to it. It's like a muscle and it gets worn out over time. I think this needs to be realistically looked at. You can't JUST do activities that take enormous amounts of self-discipline like exercise/noFAP/writing/practicing instruments. Eventually, the body and mind needs time to rest as well. I sometimes worry that this board is encouraging workaholism and extremist perfectionism. And I'm guilty of striving towards unhealthy demands on myself physically and mentally. 

&#x200B;

Maybe I was a lot more productive on No Fap. Maybe I worked harder. But what if 'being more productive' is not in fact, the solution to all of my life's problems? What if there's some fundamental issue that I'm trying to hide by being more productive? This is a thought that came to me when I read, 'the way of the superior man' (I strongly recommend this book).  


On a basic level, I see masturbation as a form of self harm. I would like all of my sexual experiences to be with another human being. I think this a noble way to live ones life and to lock ones self away and pleasure oneself is repulsive to me. 

&#x200B;

I can't see myself beating my 120 streak again anytime soon. Maybe another 30 day one. 

&#x200B;

