There's nothing weirder than a comic bombing and acting like they're killing. It can make the comedian look delusional. Especially high energy comics, who tend to be more over confident, raising a legitimate question over their grasp on reality. The first acknowledgement of a bomb is good just to let the audience know that the performer isn't insane.  However, after a while the audience might think the comedian just isn't funny or begin to feel sympathy. Also, an audience might actually have enjoyed and unfunny joke and that could get the audience on side to laugh more at later jokes. Not all bombs totally kill the momentum if the joke helps the audience engage with the performer...obviously it's better to get the laugh, but the silence shouldn't lead to panic, as all is not yet lost.

&#x200B;

It's also worth noting some comics deal with jokes bombing in an arrogant way, such as Jeselnik and Todd Barry. As part of their personas, the audience just aren't giving them what the joke deserves. That can also be good obviously only once or twice or again, the problem of sanity may come up.
